WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Imagine this scenario.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.180
You're running an
analytics workload

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.150
in Google Cloud that reads
data from Google Cloud Storage

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.880
and processes it
using a Dataflow job.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.060
The amount of data that the
job processes has been growing,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.600
but your pipeline hasn't
had any issues keeping up.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.270
You're having lunch when you
get an alert on your phone.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.030
Your pipeline is returning
processing errors.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.160
You abandon your lunch,
open your laptop,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.660
and start investigating.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.240
Sure enough, the
Dataflow job is failing.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.460
And it turns out that
Cloud Storage requests

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.300
are resulting in 429 Too
Many Requests errors.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.980
This has never happened before.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.390
What could be going on?

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.340
Well, it's possible
you've exhausted

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.520
a quota in your project, and
the job is being throttled.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.390
My name is Yuri.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.700
And I'm a site reliability
engineer at Google.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.670
Today I'm going to show you
how to proactively monitor

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.210
quotas and get notified when you
start to approach the limit so

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.550
that you can raise the quota and
avoid problems like this one.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.420
Welcome to "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.222
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.320
OK, let's dig into
this and figure out

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.269
if the errors you are
seeing are actually

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.210
due to running out of quota.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.950
We can start on the Quotas page.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.800
Here we can see that we've run
out of Egress bandwidth quota

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.820
in this region.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.750
Let's go to the Products menu
and select Metrics Explorer

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.010
under Monitoring.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.240
Here we can plot quota usage
against quota limit over time.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.490
Both of these metrics are
available for the Consumer

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.500
Quota resource.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.290
The usage metric is
called rate quota usage.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.510
Rate quotas are typically
used for limiting

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.480
the number of requests you
can make to an API or service

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.970
and are applied over a
specific time interval.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:41.640
In this case, the quota is
Egress bandwidth per second.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.120
The other kind of quota
is an allocation quota,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.980
which restricts how
many resources you

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.380
can have provisioned at a
time, such as the number

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.060
of VMs in a project.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.820
They reset when the resources
are released, when the VMs are

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.790
deleted, for example.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.520
The other metric we need here
is the quota limit metric,

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.680
which tells us what the
limit for this rate usage is.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.810
There's another way
to view this data.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.810
We can use MQL, the
Monitoring Query Language,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.800
to actually calculate a ratio
of quota usage to quota limit.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.230
We'll post a link for this
query in the episode notes.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.020
Here we clearly see that
our usage to limit ratio

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.060
exceeded 1 for a period of time.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.690
This explains why our
job was throttled.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.660
So now we know that we did
exhaust network Egress quota.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.780
And that's why our job got
throttled and started failing.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.430
We submitted a quota
increase request

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:31.780
which unblocked our job.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.630
Our pipeline is
back up and running.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.580
We don't want this to
keep happening, right?

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.530
Let's set up an alert
that will notify us

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:42.530
if we get close to the
new increased quota.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.300
Let's start on the
Alerting screen

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.430
and create a new
alerting policy.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.430
We'll use MQL to
define this policy.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:50.990
Paste the query here.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:52.940
And run it to evaluate
both the query

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.550
we've written to calculate
the usage-limit ratio

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.530
and the threshold for
that ratio that we want

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.050
to use to trigger the alert.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.660
That threshold is defined
here, in the last statement

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:05.350
in the query, using a
condition statement.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:06.725
Looks like our
signal is correct.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.330
We need to name our condition.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.040
And specify how we
want to be notified

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.110
the next time the alert fires.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.390
We also want to specify
whether the incident should

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:25.690
be automatically closed after
some amount of time passes.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:26.680
Let's name the alert.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.820
And review the
policy to validate

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.250
that we've defined our signal
and threshold correctly.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.470
And I think we did.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.220
And that's how you can use
MQL to calculate the usage

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.230
to limit ratio and
set up alerting

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.390
to be notified when you
start approaching a quota

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.210
limit in your project.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:50.710
Great.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.660
Now, if we start to
get close to the quota,

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.610
we'll get an alert so
we can avoid having

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.080
our job be throttled again.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.800
That's engineering
for reliability.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.370
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.760
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.760
on more "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:07.690
See you soon.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.740
